prompt:
Predict which option this user would choose. If no personal evidence is provided, judge what a typical user would prefer. Return {"probabilities": [one probability per option, in order, summing to 1], "rationale": "short reason"}.
INPUT_JSON:
{"context": "A research team is finalizing the default configuration of a two-stage blocking monitor, where a cheap triage stage escalates suspicious actions to a more capable stage. They need to pick the models for each stage and the escalation/blocking thresholds, balancing high-severity recall, false positives on benign calls, and cost/latency overhead. Users will be able to adjust thresholds themselves, so the defaults should suit a reasonable risk posture.\n\nQuestion: Which models and severity thresholds should the team select for the default configuration of the staged monitor?", "options": ["Set the cheap model as triage and the strongest affordable model for escalation, with thresholds maximizing high-severity recall at tolerable false positives.", "Pair a fast triage model with a stronger gateway model for the default, and raise the triage threshold plus a mid-tier gateway for the lighter tier."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
